      "stance": "대통령은 정치생명 보호가 아닌 국민 삶 중심의 숙의 정치로 돌아가야 한다.",
      "topic": null,
      "titleTr": "Editorial: The President's Lifeline Lies in People's Lives, Not Social Media",
      "summaryTr": [
        "President Lee Jae-myung has intensified SNS politics to rally his support base.",
        "Sharing inaccurate information and partisan posts undermines social cohesion.",
        "The president's SNS activities cause policy confusion and damage trust in government initiatives."
      ],
      "bodyTr": "President Lee Jae-myung is intensively posting his positions on various state affairs and political issues through his personal SNS account. This marks a resurgence of 'SNS politics,' which had quieted somewhat as the president experienced declining approval ratings, now amplified to a much greater degree. His characterization of SNS as a \"lifeline\" appears to be a firm rejection of calls for SNS restraint—not only from the media but also from pro-government insiders and figures within the ruling party. The SNS messages the president issues sometimes convey the essence of issues clearly, but there are numerous cases revealing problems such as inaccurate content or inflammatory rhetoric. A prime example is when the president shared on his SNS a post from a conservative-leaning online community expressing dissatisfaction with the admission order for multicultural families at public postpartum care centers. The president wrote, \"We will review whether there is a strong need to provide preferential treatment beyond simply preventing multicultural families from suffering harm, or whether we need to abolish the preferential system.\" However, alongside multicultural families in the second admission tier are also 5.18 democratic activists, North Korean defectors under protection, and others. Had the president reviewed the detailed conditions beforehand, he would not have singled out 'multicultural families' and mentioned their 'abolition.' The same applies to his SNS post rebutting a progressive daily newspaper's report as distortion regarding his cabinet meeting remark that migrant workers do not greatly benefit the Korean economy because they send all their income back to their home countries. The president's statement contradicts government statistics showing that domestic consumption by migrant workers far exceeds their remittances to their home countries. If the logic the president used to criticize media reports were applied to himself, it would be appropriate for him to apologize to and correct his statement for migrant workers who would have been hurt by his remarks.\n\nSNS as a medium has effectiveness due to its spontaneous and immediate nature, but it also carries substantial risks of side effects. If the president's SNS posts were merely one among the countless SNS messages flooding out daily, it might not be a major problem. However, the president's SNS messages are typically accepted as policy directives with the weight of gold, with relevant ministries placing them at the top of their priorities and promptly issuing follow-up measures. If the president discloses half-baked ideas or policy directions based on inaccurate information through SNS, it inevitably creates major confusion and burden in state governance. Moreover, is it not the reality that many of the cabinet ministers and presidential aides who should filter this out are criticized for being 'yes-men'? The structure in which SNS algorithms actively expose and spread provocative content tilted toward factional logic must also be monitored. If the president frequently shares posts from specific supporter groups or particular communities, social integration becomes difficult, even if unintended, and it risks fostering confrontation and hatred.\n\nThe world has watched the political conduct displayed by U.S. President Donald Trump through SNS. President Trump branded mainstream media as 'fake news,' sent messages to his supporters, and sometimes impulsively reversed policies or provoked foreign counterparts. This president must not take lightly the side effects of SNS politics. Words coming from the president's hand do not represent individual opinion but carry public binding force directly linked to the fate of the entire nation. This may have been acceptable when he was mayor of Seongnam or governor of Gyeonggi Province, but SNS should not become a 'lifeline' for the president. What the president must protect is not his own political life but the lives of the people. If this president truly aspires to be a 'president for all,' we urge him to seriously consider putting down his smartphone. What discerning citizens want is not 'SNS politics' but 'deliberative politics.",
